## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest · **Tests:** 10 · **Production methods covered:** 10/10

### Summary

| Severity | Count |
|----------|-------|
| 🔴 Critical | 3 (affecting all 10 tests) |
| 🟡 Medium | 1 |
| Total | 4 distinct anti-patterns |

This test class is a textbook example of **coverage touching** — every public method on `ProductCatalog` is called, achieving high line coverage, but almost nothing is actually verified. 5 tests have **zero assertions**; the other 5 use only `Assert.IsNotNull`, which is trivially true for the types returned (non-nullable `List<T>`, `string`, and a just-added product). These tests **cannot catch regressions**.

---

### 🔴 Critical Findings

#### 1. Coverage Touching (systemic — all 10 tests)

The class walks every public member of `ProductCatalog` in declaration order (`Add`, `Get`, `UpdatePrice`, `Remove`, `Search`, `GetAll`, `GetProductCount`, `ApplyBulkDiscount`, `ExportToCsv`, `ImportFromCsv`) with no meaningful verification. This inflates coverage to 95% while proving nothing about correctness.

#### 2. No Assertions (5 tests)

| Test | Line | What goes unverified |
|------|------|----------------------|
| `AddProduct_Works` | 17 | Product was actually persisted |
| `UpdatePrice_Works` | 33 | Price was actually changed to 14.99 |
| `RemoveProduct_Works` | 39 | Product was actually deleted |
| `GetProductCount_Works` | 64 | Count value is never checked |
| `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` | 69 | Discounted prices are never checked |
| `ImportFromCsv_Works` | 85 | Imported product is never verified |

**Example fix — `AddProduct_Works`:**
```csharp
// BEFORE (line 17-21)
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_Works()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);
}

// AFTER
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_StoresProductRetrievableBySku()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);

    var retrieved = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual("Widget", retrieved?.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, retrieved?.Price);
}
```

**Example fix — `UpdatePrice_Works`:**
```csharp
// BEFORE (line 33-36)
[TestMethod]
public void UpdatePrice_Works()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
}

